# [[.Name]]

A starter Goa / Goa-AI service created by `highbay init` or the `init_project`
MCP tool, with a starter evaluator suite that `highbay verify` and `verify_change`
can run against it.

## What is here

- `design/` declares the `[[.Name]]` service. `ask` (POST /ask) runs the
  `helper` agent on a question. `price` (GET /prices/{item}) returns an item's
  price in cents. The agent can call `price` through its `lookup_price` tool.
  `override_prompt` (PUT /prompts/{prompt_id}) replaces the helper's system
  prompt text until the process exits; it requires the key in the
  `HIGHBAY_LIVE_TARGET_KEY` environment variable, which Highbay sets for a live
  target, and rejects every call without one. `key` is left optional in
  `design/design.go` so a missing `X-Live-Target-Key` header reaches
  `Service.APIKeyAuth` and returns `401` (`unauthorized`) rather than `400`
  (`missing_field`) from the HTTP decoder; if you pass a security credential
  field to tools with `Inject(...)` (which requires the payload field to be in
  `Required(...)`), check for a missing header in HTTP middleware before the
  generated decoder runs when you want missing headers to answer `401`.
  `design/model.go` declares the C4 architecture model. `design/story.go`
  declares the Story: who uses the service and the flows their requests take
  (see "Change checkpoints" below).
- `gen/` is generated from `design/`. The guide edits the next design in its
  draft and proposes a revision for you to adopt. The builder calls the
  `generate_code` MCP tool on the Highbay project server to write that agreed
  design into its working copy's `design/` and regenerate `gen/`; it edits
  neither directory. An operator can run `highbay gen`
  in this directory: it runs Goa's generator for this module with the
  module's own versions. Never edit `gen/`. Generation compiles the design only, so update the
  hand-written code in `internal/` and `cmd/` to match it; the builder then builds and tests
  the uncommitted files with `check_project`, as described in "Next steps".
  `AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md` is generated with it and maps the agent, its tools
  and the generated packages for coding agents; it names the same Highbay tools
  as "Next steps" for generating, checking and verifying this project.
- `internal/helper` is the agent's planner and `internal/service` implements the
  service. `Service.Ask` builds an in-memory runtime and store per question
  because this starter has no multi-turn sessions or `Confirmation` tools, and
  `Service.Price` returns expected outcomes as a result value (`known: false`)
  because errors returned by bound service methods that do not implement
  `planner.ToolFailureProvider` end the agent run. If you add a tool with
  `Confirmation` (which pauses the run before the bound method's own
  authorization runs; the host can also deny unauthorized calls immediately
  when handling the suspension), build one runtime and store in `main` so
  paused runs can be resumed, as Highbay's `examples/orders` reference service
  does.
- `cmd/[[.Name]]` serves the API. The agent runs in memory and sends every
  model call to a goa-ai model gateway given with `-model-gateway host:port`;
  the gateway holds the model provider and its API keys.
- `eval/` is a separate Go module: the starter evaluator suite. It runs the
  `[[.Name]]` binary and serves a scripted model (not an
  LLM) through its own model gateway. Its four scenarios are:
  - `prices_tea`: asks "How much is tea?" and checks that the agent called
    `lookup_price` and answered from that call's result;
  - `prices_cookie`: asks "How much is cookie?" and checks the same for
    cookie. It is the test the Story's finished flow `prices-cookie`
    requires: the Story table `eval/design/story_tests.go` declares it, and
    `PricesCookie` in `eval/acceptance` implements it;
  - `unknown_item`: asks "How much is coffee?", which the
    catalog does not price, and checks that the agent calls `lookup_price`
    and reports that it found no price;
  - `prompt_override_reaches_model`: checks that `override_prompt` rejects a
    missing or wrong key and that the next question's model request carries the
    new prompt text.

  Without stored verification settings, a live target uses the tests Highbay
  accepted for that build. Their `--live` mode starts `[[.Name]]` for that
  change, using the model chosen in the Live target view through Highbay's model
  gateway, without receiving provider settings. A changed model choice applies
  to targets started afterwards; a running target keeps its model. Its program,
  `eval/gen/cmd/[[.Name]]-evals`, is generated with the rest of `eval/gen/`
  from `eval/design/` and calls `eval/acceptance`: its `Name`, `Hooks`,
  `New`, `Live` and `LiveThroughGateway`. When a build runs with a test
  writer (without stored verification settings), `eval/` (`eval/design/`
  included) belongs to the test writer, and Highbay regenerates `eval/gen/` when
  the writer reads an unread assignment and at each report; after changing `eval/design/`, the test
  writer reports to have Highbay generate it again or runs `highbay gen -dir eval`
  first. If you edit `eval/design/` yourself
  outside a build, regenerate its code with `highbay gen -dir eval` (or have the
  builder call `generate_code` with `target: "eval"`).
  The test writer runs every Go command with `GOWORK=off` and does not commit:
  Highbay commits the tests it accepts.
  `eval/design/story_tests.go` lists the tests the Story requires, one
  scenario per finished flow, such as `prices_cookie`. `highbay init` wrote it
  from `design/story.go`, and Highbay writes it again from the agreed Story when
  the test writer reads an unread assignment and at each report; leave it as it is.
  Regenerating `eval/` keeps those tests. If you store verification settings
  for another suite, no build writes it again, and it is yours to keep or
  delete.

The evaluator suite lives in this repository, so anyone who can read the code
can read its scenarios; none of them is hidden. Keep scenarios that must stay
hidden from builders in an evaluator repository they cannot read. The
builder's programs run with your file access, so while a run executes they
could read that repository's files on this machine.

## Next steps

If you created this project with `highbay init`, make the first commit and start
Highbay in this directory:

```sh
git init
git add .gitignore README.md AGENTS_QUICKSTART.md go.mod go.sum design gen internal cmd eval
git commit -m "Start [[.Name]] from highbay init"
highbay
```

`highbay` starts this project's server, or attaches to it when it is already
running, and opens the project's views page in your browser. If the guide
created this project with `init_project`, the Highbay project server has already
initialized the Git repository, created the initial commit (`commit` in
`init_project`'s result) and bound itself to this directory (`dir`).

For each change:

1. The guide calls `update_draft` before drafting a new change, writes only
   in the draft folder it returns and corrects the files it reports as needing
   correction there. When a file to correct is in the project folder, the
   guide tells you what to correct and waits: it never edits the project
   folder. It calls `update_draft` again until none remain, then calls
   `refresh_story`, waits for success and uses that evaluated draft with
   `propose_change`, or `revise_change` when the change is refined.
   You confirm it on the views page, which
   also shows its architecture preview (`preview_architecture`). Then you start
   its build there: Highbay gives the builder its assignment and, without stored
   verification settings, adds a test writer, which writes the tests the
   change's Story requires and reports them for Highbay to check and accept.
2. The builder reads its new assignment before working in its own project
   copy and stays on the branch checked out there. It runs `git status`;
   files left by earlier work must not be committed into this change. It
   calls `generate_code` to write the agreed `design/` and regenerate `gen/`,
   then edits the hand-written service code. In a project that
   builds with a test writer (without stored verification settings), the test
   module `eval/` (`eval/design/` included) belongs to the test writer: the
   builder leaves `eval/` untouched, and if the Story (`design/story.go`) or
   the test module's contract needs a change, asks you so the guide can draft
   and you can confirm a revised change. The builder edits neither `design/`
   nor `gen/`. When its assignment's `gowork_off` is true, it prefixes every
   Go command with `GOWORK=off`.
3. The builder calls `check_project` to build and test its uncommitted files
   in Linux containers (Docker required), leaving `eval/` out when the project
   has no stored verification settings (or testing both modules when stored
   verification settings are configured). When it cannot download dependencies
   and its output shows a missing `go.sum` or `go.work.sum` entry or a `go.mod`
   update, it calls `update_dependencies` (which likewise leaves `eval/` out
   when the project has no stored verification settings) and checks again.
   `read_check_output` returns the captured output, page by page, for the
   `op_id` in either result.
4. The builder commits on its own branch, then passes the new commit ID as `candidate_commit`
   with the confirmed change's `change_id` to `start_live_target`, and also
   its `request_revision` to `verify_change`. Without stored verification
   settings, both use the tests Highbay accepted for that build; choose a model
   in the Live target view before the builder starts a target.
5. Without stored verification settings, `verify_change` is refused until
   Highbay has accepted the test writer's tests. When a verification passes, you
   review its result and approve it on the views page; Highbay merges and
   deploys nothing.

See the
[MCP tools reference](https://github.com/goadesign/highbay/blob/main/docs/REFERENCE.md#mcp-tools)
for each tool's inputs and results.

`highbay verify` and `highbay approve` are operator tools that check a commit
outside the server. To verify the current commit against the starter
evaluator suite yourself:

```sh
VERIFY_DIR="$(mktemp -d)"
WORK_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/work"
REPORT_DIR="$VERIFY_DIR/reports"
highbay verify \
  -candidate-repo . -candidate-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -binary [[.Name]]=./cmd/[[.Name]] \
  -suite-repo . -suite-commit "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" -suite-module eval -suite-command ./gen/cmd/[[.Name]]-evals \
  -request-revision starter -work-dir "$WORK_DIR" -report-dir "$REPORT_DIR" -builder-view
```

`highbay verify` keeps `$WORK_DIR` after the run: it holds the clones, the
built binaries, and the evaluator's private evidence, including each build's
log and each step's output in `$WORK_DIR/evidence/`. `-report-dir "$REPORT_DIR"`
always writes the full verification report outside the repository as
`$REPORT_DIR/<digest>.json` (used by `highbay approve`), while `-builder-view` hides holdout scenario names and checks
in the completed report JSON and summary line (if a run fails before writing a
report, `stderr` prints operator setup/execution failure diagnostics). Do not
share `$WORK_DIR`, `$REPORT_DIR`, or setup/execution failure output with coding
agents that build the service.

`highbay verify` checks a commit. The builder runs the `check_project` loop in
"Next steps" above before it commits.

## Change checkpoints

The guide writes only in the draft folder returned by `update_draft` or
`refresh_story`, never in the project folder. It calls `refresh_story` to
evaluate that draft and passes its succeeded `op_id` as `story_refresh_op_id`
when proposing or revising a Change Card. The card saves that evaluated design
and Story, not later project-folder edits. The saved source contributes to
`request_revision`; callers do not upload it.
An adopted Story remains paired with its source when a later revision omits
`story_refresh_op_id`.

The Story is `design/story.go`. It ships with one finished flow,
`prices-cookie`, and with the test that flow requires: the method
`PricesCookie` of package `eval/acceptance`. To describe new behavior, the
guide adds a flow to the draft's `design/story.go` and calls `refresh_story`. When you start a
build of the change, each finished flow of its Story is a test the build
requires, and the test writer adds the missing methods beside `PricesCookie`.

To try prompt wording on a running `[[.Name]]` before it is verified, the
builder calls `start_live_target` with the confirmed change's `change_id` and
the full candidate commit ID, then
`draft_override` with `prompt_id` `[[.Name]].helper.system`. To keep a draft,
the builder writes the text into `internal/helper/planner.go`, runs
`check_project`, commits, and calls `verify_change` with the new commit ID and
the confirmed Change Card's `request_revision`. Without stored verification
settings, wait for Highbay to accept the build's tests and choose a model in
the Live target view before starting the target. With stored settings, the
operator's evaluator starts it without accepted test-writer tests.
